{"id": 3, "ts": 1780543801.7612004, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "emily_brooks", "message": "I\u2019d like to put Candidate\u202fB forward as the current leading option.\u202fHe is described as very conscientious, which speaks directly to operational reliability, and he handles stress very well\u2014both critical for long\u2011distance flights that can involve extended periods of high workload and unexpected weather.\u202fHis strong ability to assess weather conditions and his excellent computer skills also indicate solid technical competence for modern cockpit systems.\u202fWhile I acknowledge the concern that he can come across as pretentious and sometimes adopts the wrong tone, these communication style issues seem less severe than the clear reliability and stress\u2011resilience strengths he brings, especially given the safety\u2011critical nature of the role.", "thoughts": "We need to produce first public message and metadata JSON. No prior discussion, so we need to start. Should we pick a candidate? We need to argue. We have notes for all candidates. Need to start discussion, maybe propose a candidate based on evidence. Let's evaluate quickly:\n\nCriteria: reliability, stress, technical competence, decision quality, attention, cooperation, communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nCandidate A: can anticipate dangerous situations, see complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership, but unfriendly, eats unhealthily. Leadership good, anticipation good for safety, but unfriendly may affect crew cooperation, communication? No mention of stress handling. So mixed.\n\nCandidate B: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, but regarded as pretentious, sometimes adopts wrong tone. Conscientious = reliability, stress good, technical (computer), but communication tone issues.\n\nCandidate C: can make correct decisions quickly, difficulty communicating ideas, rega